p whitespace.\n        - Handle parentheses for negative values: if it starts with `(` and ends with `)`, remove them and negate the number.\n        - Remove `$` and `,`.\n        - Convert to float.\n        - If parsing fails or amount is empty, skip the row.\n    f. Add the amount to the category's total in the `totals` dictionary.\n    g. Ensure the total is rounded to 2 decimals.\n\nWait, the `csv` module is probably the best way to handle the quoted fields.\n\nLet's refine the amount parsing:\n- Remove `$`.\n- Remove `,`.\n- Check for `(...)` pattern.\n- Convert to float.\n\nWait, if I use `csv.reader`, I can handle the quotes.\n\n```python\nimport csv\nimport io\n\ndef summarize_expenses(csv_text):\n    totals = {}\n    # Using io.StringIO to treat the string as a file\n    f = io.StringIO(csv_text.strip())\n    reader = csv.reader(f)\n    \n    try:\n        header = next(reader)\n    except StopIteration:\n        return {}\n\n    # Identify indices for category and amount\n    # The test assumes index 1 is category and 2 is amount.\n    # header = ['date', 'category', 'amount']\n    \n    for row in reader:\n        if not row:\n            continue\n        \n        # According to requirements:\n        # Category at index 1, amount at index 2\n        if len(row) < 3:\n            continue\n            \n        cat_raw = row[1]\n        amount_raw = row[2]\n        \n        # Clean category\n        category = cat_raw.strip().lower()\n        \n        # Clean amount\n        amount_str = amount_raw.strip().replace('$', '').replace(',', '')\n        \n        if not amount_str:\n            continue\n            \n        try:\n            # Handle accounting negative notation: (12.50) -> -12.50\n            if amount_str.startswith('(') and amount_str.endswith(')'):\n                amount_str = '-' + amount_str[1:-1]\n            \n            amount = float(amount_str)\n            \n            totals[category] = totals.get(category, 0) + amount\